
{"id":"af328de3-0e65-4748-af30-69eac0c5768a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785027725,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the spec. The task is to parse and validate interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation records. The case payload has \"lines\" array of strings. We need to parse each line, expecting a pattern: \"Thought: ...\", \"Action: ...\", \"Observation: ...\", \"Final: ...\". Also possibly \"Action Input: ...\"? The spec mentions Action Input but not in the example lines. However, the candidate_artifact mentions \"Action Input\". We need to handle that as part of the action? The spec says: \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" and \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" Also \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" So we need to validate order: The sequence should be cycles of Thought, Action, Observation, and then maybe Final at the end. Also note that Action Input might be part of the Action line? Or a separate line? The examples show \"Action: lookup\" and \"Thought: inspect\", etc. There is no separate Action Input line. But the description mentions \"Action Input\". Possibly it's part of the Action record, like \"Action: lookup\\nAction Input: ...\" but that's not in lines. Alternatively, maybe the lines can contain \"Action Input:\" as a separate line after Action. We need to infer from typical ReAct format. The problem statement says: \"Parse and validate interleaved Thought, Action, Action Input, and Observation records\". So we must treat Action Input as a possible line type. But the fixtures only show Thought, Action, Observation, Final. However, we need to handle general cases. The output schema requires trajectory array of objects with type and content. So we parse each line, identify its type based on prefix. If a line starts with \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", \"Action Input:\", \"Observation:\", or \"Final:\", then we extract the content after colon and space? Or just after colon? We'll assume format: \"Type: content\". The content is the rest of the line after the prefix and colon.\n\nWe need to validate order: The sequence must follow: Thought -> Action -> Observation (repeating) and optionally Final at the end, but Final can only appear at the end and must not be followed by anything. Also after a Final, no more lines. Also any line that is not one of those types is invalid? Probably treat as error. Also Action Input is optional? Probably it comes after Action before Observation. The spec says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" It doesn't mention Action Input as a required step. So Action Input is an optional element that can appear after Action and before Observation? Or it can be part of Action? Usually in ReAct, action input is part of the action. But here it's a separate record. We'll treat it as an optional record that can appear after Action and must be followed by Observation? But the spec says \"Action Input\" is a record type. To handle this, we need to define the valid turn order. The problem statement says: \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" It doesn't mention Action Input in the cycle. Possibly Action Input is optional and can appear after Action before Observation. But then the cycle becomes Thought -> Action -> [Action Input] -> Observation. But then the next cycle starts with Thought. Also Final is allowed only at the end. Also need to handle \"reasoning boundary\" - Final only at reasoning boundary. Not clear, but we treat Final as end.\n\nAlso there is \"stop grammar\" and \"tool schema\" - but we don't have action schemas. So we just validate order and types.\n\nWe need to return error_line (one-based line index of first violation, or null if valid), trajectory (list of {type:string, content:string} for parsed records up to the violation? Actually the spec: \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So trajectory should contain only the valid records before the violation. And valid flag false if violation, true otherwise. If valid, trajectory includes all records.\n\nAlso note: The output schema requires trajectory array of objects with \"type\" and \"content\". Type must be lowercase? The spec says \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records\". So we convert type to lowercase: \"thought\", \"action\", \"action_input\", \"observation\", \"final\". Also content should be the string after prefix trimmed. We'll strip leading/trailing whitespace? Probably keep as is.\n\nValidation rules:\n- Each line must start with one of the prefixes: \"Thought:\", \"Action:\", \"Action Input:\", \"Observation:\", \"Final:\" (case-sensitive? Probably case-sensitive as per the examples). We'll assume exact match of prefix, including colon. After colon, there may be a space. We'll strip the prefix and then take the rest as content, trimming leading spaces? But the content might be empty. We'll treat as empty string if nothing after colon.\n- Order: Must follow pattern: start with Thought. Then after Thought, must be Action (or optionally Action Input? Actually the cycle is Thought->Action->Observation. But if Action Input appears, it should come after Action and before Observation. So we need to define state machine: We'll track expected next types.\n- The sequence is a series of \"cycles\": each cycle consists of one Thought, one Action, optionally one Action Input, then one Observation. Then repeat.\n- At any point, we can end with a Final, but only if the current state is after a Thought? Or after Observation? The spec says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" Reasoning boundary could be after Observation? Or after Thought? Typically Final is after Thought (since Final is a reasoning step). But it says \"at a reasoning boundary\". Possibly after a Thought or after an Observation? We need to infer. The example \"Final: done\" appears after Thought and Action and Observation? Actually in first example, the lines are: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Final. So there is a Thought before Final. So Final can follow Thought. In that case, the cycle is incomplete because after Thought, we expect Action, but instead we get Final. That is allowed only at the end. So Final can appear after a Thought, ending the sequence. Similarly, can it appear after Observation? Possibly yes if it's the end. But the spec says \"only at a reasoning boundary\" which might be after a reasoning step (Thought or Final itself). Let's adopt: Final can only appear after a Thought or after an Observation? To be safe, we allow Final after any record that is part of a valid cycle? But the spec says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" I'll interpret that Final can only appear as the last line, and it must follow either a Thought or an Observation (i.e., after a reasoning step, not after Action or Action Input). Because Action is not a reasoning step. So we'll enforce: Final is allowed only as the last line, and the previous line must be either Thought or Observation (not Action or Action Input). But also Final cannot be followed by anything.\n\nNow, what constitutes a violation? We need to find the first line that breaks the expected order. Also if a line has invalid prefix (not one of those), that's a violation. Also if Final appears not at end or after invalid preceding, that's a violation.\n\nWe process lines sequentially. Keep a state machine with expected next types. We'll define a set of valid types. For each line, we parse type and content. Then check if type is allowed in current state. If not, record error_line = line index (1-based), and trajectory includes all records processed so far that were valid and up to but not including the invalid line? The spec: \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So trajectory should contain the records from lines that are valid before the violation. So we add each parsed record to trajectory only if it passes validation. When we encounter a violation, we stop adding, set error_line, and return with valid=false. If no violation, error_line=null, valid=true, trajectory includes all.\n\nBut note: What about duplicate or missing records? For example, if we get two Thoughts in a row, that's a violation because after Thought we expect Action. Similarly, if we get Action without preceding Thought, violation. Also after Observation, we expect Thought (next cycle). So state transitions:\n- Initial state: expect Thought or Final? Actually Final can be first? The spec says Final only at the end, so first line cannot be Final. So initial expect Thought.\n- After Thought: expect Action or Final (since Final allowed after Thought). But also Action Input? No, Action Input only after Action. So after Thought, expect Action (or Final).\n- After Action: expect Action Input or Observation? We have to decide. Since Action Input is optional, after Action we can have Action Input or Observation. So after Action, expect Action_Input or Observation.\n- After Action_Input: expect Observation (since Action Input must be followed by Observation).\n- After Observation: expect Thought (next cycle) or Final? Final allowed after Observation? Possibly yes, reasoning boundary. So after Observation, expect Thought or Final.\n- After Final: nothing expected, but if more lines, violation.\n\nAlso need to handle that after Final, we stop; no more lines.\n\nAlso if we get a line that is not one of the five types, violation.\n\nNow, what about the \"Action Input\" line? Can it appear without an Action before? No. So after before Action, Action Input is not allowed.\n\nWe'll implement a state machine. Let's denote states as strings: \"START\" (expect Thought or maybe Final? But Final not allowed at start, so expect Thought), \"THOUGHT\", \"ACTION\", \"ACTION_INPUT\", \"OBSERVATION\", \"FINAL\" (after final we terminate).\n\nBut we also need to handle Final after Thought, Action? Actually after thought we allow Final. So state after Thought can go to FINAL if we see Final line. But then we need to ensure that after Final, we stop. So we can treat Final as a terminal state.\n\nWe'll parse each line, determine its type. Then check if the type is allowed in the current expected set. If yes, transition to new state accordingly. If not, violation.\n\nWe'll maintain a list of parsed records (type, content). For each valid line, we append to trajectory. When violation, we break.\n\nEdge cases: The line might have empty content? That's allowed. Content may have leading/trailing spaces? We'll strip? The content should be the string after the colon and optional space. But we need to preserve exact content? The schema requires string. We can keep the content exactly as after colon, trimming only the colon and any leading space? The examples show \"Thought: inspect\" -> content \"inspect\". So likely we strip leading spaces after colon. But to be safe, we'll split at first colon and take the rest, then strip leading whitespace. But if there are multiple colons, only first matters. We'll use prefix splitting: line.startswith(\"Thought:\") etc. Then content = line[len(prefix):].strip()? But if content has leading spaces, we strip? The examples don't have spaces. We'll strip leading and trailing whitespace, because it's typical. But the spec says \"Return lowercase typed trajectory records and an explicit validity flag.\" It doesn't specify content trimming. I think it's safe to strip.\n\nNow, we need to return JSON-serializable object as per schema. We'll implement solve(case) that returns dict with keys: valid, error_line, trajectory. error_line is integer or None (null). Note: JSON null is None in Python.\n\nWe must not mutate case. We'll extract lines from case (keys: \"lines\", possibly \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore nonce). The payload may have _case_nonce but we don't need it.\n\nWe'll raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if case doesn't have \"lines\" or it's not a list, we should raise ValueError. But the fixture shows payload with lines. We'll check.\n\nAlso, we must handle empty lines? If lines is empty list, that is valid? There's no trajectory, error_line null, valid true? Possibly. But the format requires at least one thought? Not sure. But we can handle: empty lines -> valid? But then no records. The spec says \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" With no lines, there are no records, so it's valid. But then trajectory empty. We'll treat as valid.\n\nNow, the state machine: We'll define allowed_next dict mapping from current state to set of allowed types.\n\nInitial state: we have no current state. We'll set expected types based on whether we are in a cycle. Actually we can track the last type processed. We'll implement as:\n\nInitialize state = \"START\". Allowed from START: \"thought\" only? Also \"final\"? No, final not allowed at start. So allowed = {\"thought\"}.\nIf line type is \"thought\", set state = \"THOUGHT\".\nFrom \"THOUGHT\": allowed = {\"action\", \"final\"}.\nFrom \"action\": allowed = {\"action_input\", \"observation\"}.\nFrom \"action_input\": allowed = {\"observation\"}.\nFrom \"observation\": allowed = {\"thought\", \"final\"}.\nFrom \"final\": no allowed (should not have more lines). But we will treat after final as a terminal state: any further line is violation. So we can set state = \"FINAL\" and allowed = set().\n\nBut note: \"final\" is a type, not a state. We'll treat state as the last record type. Actually after processing a final line, we still have state as \"FINAL\". Then next line check: allowed from \"FINAL\" is empty, so any next line is violation.\n\nNow, we need to consider that after a violation, we stop and return with the valid prefix before the violation. So we don't add the violating line to trajectory.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'lines' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing 'lines'\")\n    lines = case['lines']\n    if not isinstance(lines, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: 'lines' not a list\")\n    # Preprocess: ensure each line is string? We'll trust input.\n\n    trajectory = []\n    error_line = None\n    # define mapping from prefix to type name lowercase\n    prefix_map = {\n        'Thought:': 'thought',\n        'Action:': 'action',\n        'Action Input:': 'action_input',\n        'Observation:': 'observation',\n        'Final:': 'final'\n    }\n    # state transitions\n    allowed_next = {\n        None: {'thought'},  # initial state (no previous)\n        'thought': {'action', 'final'},\n        'action': {'action_input', 'observation'},\n        'action_input': {'observation'},\n        'observation': {'thought', 'final'},\n        'final': set()\n    }\n\n    last_type = None  # None means before any line\n\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):\n        # parse line\n        parsed_type = None\n        parsed_content = None\n        found = False\n        for prefix, type_name in prefix_map.items():\n            if line.startswith(prefix):\n                parsed_type = type_name\n                parsed_content = line[len(prefix):].strip()\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            # invalid line\n            error_line = idx + 1\n            break\n\n        # check if type allowed from current last_type\n        allowed = allowed_next[last_type]  # last_type is None for first line, use None key\n        if parsed_type not in allowed:\n            error_line = idx + 1\n            break\n\n        # valid record, add to trajectory\n        trajectory.append({'type': parsed_type, 'content': parsed_content})\n        last_type = parsed_type\n\n        # after final, if there are more lines, they will be caught by next iteration because allowed set empty\n        # if there are no more lines, loop ends\n\n    # if loop completes without break, check if last_type is a valid ending state? The spec doesn't require that the sequence ends with a specific type. But Final is required only at the end and at reasoning boundary. If we end without Final, is it valid? The spec says \"Parse only complete Thought, Action, Observation, or Final records.\" It doesn't say that the sequence must end with Final. However, the cycle might be incomplete? For instance, if the last line is an Action without Observation, that's incomplete cycle. Should that be valid? The spec says \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" So a nonterminal cycle must have all three. If the sequence ends mid-cycle, it's incomplete and should be invalid? The problem statement says \"On the first violation return its one-based line and the valid prefix.\" So if the sequence is missing a required step, the violation occurs at the line where the next expected record is missing? But since there is no next line, the violation is at the end? The spec: \"Require Thought then Action then Observation for each nonterminal cycle.\" That implies that a cycle must be complete. If the last cycle is incomplete, it's a violation. Where is the violation line? It would be the line that breaks the expectation, i.e., the missing line. But since there is no line, we might need to consider that the sequence ends and the last record indicates that the next required record is missing. Usually in such parsers, you would mark the line that is missing, but there is no line. The spec says \"On the first violation return its one-based line\". If violation is due to missing line, there is no line. That is problematic. Perhaps we should check after processing all lines that the state is a valid ending state. What are valid ending states? The spec allows Final at the end, but also after Observation? Actually the cycle must be complete. After an Observation, the next expected is Thought or Final. If the sequence ends after an Observation, it's okay because that completes a cycle. But the cycle after that is not started. So ending after Observation is acceptable. Ending after Thought is not acceptable because that would be an incomplete cycle (missing Action). Similarly, ending after Action or Action_Input is incomplete. Also ending after Final is acceptable. So we need to validate that the final state is either 'observation' or 'final' (or possibly 'thought' if Final follows? No, if last is thought, it's incomplete). Also starting with nothing? If lines empty, it's valid? Possibly. We'll define valid end states: None (empty), \"observation\", \"final\". Also maybe \"thought\"? But no, that would be a dangling thought. So we'll enforce that after processing all lines, if the last_type is not in {\"observation\", \"final\", None}, then it's a violation. But what line number? The violation occurs at the end, after the last line. The error_line could be the last line number + 1? But that doesn't make sense. Alternatively, we could treat the violation as occurring at the line that completes the incomplete cycle? For example, if last line is Thought, the violation is that the next line should be Action but it's missing. The spec says \"first violation return its one-based line\". The first violation could be the line that is missing, but it's not a line. This is ambiguous. Looking at typical problems like this, they usually consider the sequence valid only if it ends with a Observation or Final. If it ends with Thought, it's invalid, and the error line is the line of the Thought because that creates an incomplete cycle. Similarly, if it ends with Action, error line is the Action line. So we can set error_line to the index of the last line processed (the one that caused the incomplete cycle). That is, if we finish loop without break, we then check if last_type is valid end state. If not, we set error_line = len(lines) (the last line) and trajectory stays as the records before that? But the last record is already added to trajectory. If we decide it's a violation, then the valid prefix should be up to but not including that line? However, the spec says \"valid prefix\" meaning the records before the violation. So we would need to remove that last record from trajectory. In our loop, we added the last record before checking end condition. So we could handle it by appending only after checking end condition? Or we could check end condition after loop and if invalid, then pop the last record and set error_line accordingly.\n\nAlternatively, we could incorporate the end condition into the state machine: The state after processing a thought expects action, so end-of-input is a violation because action is missing. That violation occurs at the position after the thought line, i.e., the next line number (len(lines)+1). But that is not a line. I think the safer interpretation is that the sequence must end with a complete cycle or Final, and if it doesn't, the violation is the last line that started the incomplete cycle. For example, if the last line is \"Thought: something\", that line is invalid because it doesn't have a following Action. So the violation line is that Thought line. So we can treat \"Thought\" as allowed only if followed by Action (or Final). So in the state machine, after \"thought\", the allowed set includes \"action\" and \"final\". If the input ends while in state \"thought\", that is a violation at that line. So we can implement that by after processing each line, we don't check end condition until after loop. After loop, if current state is not a valid end state, we have to backtrack: The last line is the violation. So we need to set error_line = idx+1 (where idx is the last processed index) and remove that last record from trajectory. But note that after processing that line, we had already validated it as allowed at that time (since allowed set included action and final, but at the time of processing, the line itself was allowed based on previous state). The violation is that it wasn't followed by the next required element. So the first violation occurs at the missing next line. Usually in parsing, you would detect this when looking for the next line and find none. Since there is no next line, you could say the violation is at line number (len(lines)+1) but that's unusual. I'll adopt the approach that the violation is at the last line if it's an incomplete cycle. That is, if after all lines, the last type is \"thought\", \"action\", or \"action_input\", then the last line is the violation (because it starts an incomplete cycle). We'll set error_line to len(lines) (one-based) and remove that last record from trajectory. That seems consistent with \"valid prefix\" meaning the records up to but not including the violating line.\n\nWe also need to handle the case where the sequence ends with Final, but there are additional lines after? Our loop would have caught that because after Final, allowed set empty, so if there is a next line it would be violation. So if loop completes and last_type is \"final\", it's fine.\n\nThus, after loop, if error_line is None, we check if last_type in {None, \"observation\", \"final\"}:\n- If yes, valid = True, error_line = None.\n- If no, then we have violation at the last line. Set error_line = len(lines) (one-based). Then remove the last record from trajectory (since the violating line is the last one). But careful: the last record is already in trajectory. We pop it. Then valid = False.\n\nBut note: what if the first line itself is a violation because it's not allowed? That case is already caught in loop, and error_line set to that line, trajectory is empty.\n\nOne more nuance: If there are no lines, last_type is None, which is valid end state. So valid=True, trajectory empty.\n\nNow test with the given fixtures:\n\nCase 000: lines: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Final.\nProcessing:\nidx0 Thought: allowed from None -> ok, append, last_type=thought\nidx1 Action: allowed from thought -> ok, append, last_type=action\nidx2 Observation: allowed from action -> ok, append, last_type=observation\nidx3 Thought: allowed from observation -> ok, append, last_type=thought\nidx4 Final: allowed from thought -> ok, append, last_type=final\nEnd loop, last_type=final, valid end. valid=true, error_line null. That matches expected? Probably valid.\n\nCase 001: lines: Thought, Action, Thought, Thought, Final.\nProcessing:\nidx0 Thought: ok, last_type=thought\nidx1 Action: ok, last_type=action\nidx2 Thought: from action, allowed set {'action_input','observation'} - Thought not allowed -> violation at line 3 (idx2). Trajectory so far: two records. error_line=3. That seems correct.\nCase 002 same as 001, same result.\n\nBut note: The spec says \"Permit Final only at the end and only at a reasoning boundary.\" In case 001, Final is at line 5, but we already have violation at line 3. So we never process Final. That's fine.\n\nNow, consider case where sequence ends with Action without Observation: e.g., lines: Thought, Action. After processing both, last_type=action. Loop ends, error_line=None, then we check: last_type not in valid ends -> set error_line=2 (last line), remove last record (Action) from trajectory, trajectory becomes only Thought. Then valid=false. That would indicate violation at line 2. That seems plausible: the Action line is invalid because it starts an incomplete cycle (missing Observation). But is Action line itself invalid? At the time we processed it, it was allowed (since previous was Thought). So the violation is that the required Observation is missing. The spec says \"first violation return its one-based line\". The first violation occurs at line 2 because after line 2, the expected Observation is missing. So line 2 is the offending line. So our approach is consistent.\n\nSimilarly, if sequence ends with Thought only: lines: Thought. last_type=thought, invalid end, set error_line=1, remove Thought, trajectory empty. That means the Thought line is the first violation because it requires action following. That seems correct.\n\nNow, what about Action Input? If we have Thought, Action, Action Input, Observation, that's valid. Then after Observation, can have Thought or Final. If we end after Action Input without Observation? That would be invalid, the Action Input line is the violation because it requires Observation.\n\nAlso, what about Final without preceding Thought? Should be invalid. If lines: ['Final: done'], from None allowed only {'thought'}, so Final not allowed -> violation at line 1.\n\nNow, need to also consider that lines might have other texts like \"Action: lookup\" with extra spaces? We'll handle stripping. Also note that \"Action Input:\" prefix includes space? The prefix is \"Action Input:\" (with space before Input? Actually the standard is \"Action Input:\" with a space? In examples they show \"Action:\" without space. I'll assume the prefix is exactly \"Action Input:\" including colon. So we need to match that exactly. We'll put in prefix_map as 'Action Input:'.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) with above logic. Return dict with keys valid, error_line, trajectory.\n\nEdge: The trajectory items must have \"type\" and \"content\". Type already lowercase as we used. Content could be empty string, fine.\n\nNow, we need to output as JSON string with key \"implementation\". The entire response must be exactly one JSON object. So we will produce a Python string containing the source code, then encode as JSON string. We need to escape backslashes, newlines, quotes.\n\nWe'll write the implementation as a string.\n\nMake sure to import nothing except maybe json? Actually we don't need to import anything; we only use standard types. But we might need to use json for output? No, solve returns dict, and the evaluator will serialize. So we just return dict.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) with docstring? Not required.\n\nNow, we need to generate the final JSON with \"implementation\" key. We'll produce a single-line string with escaped newlines.\n\nLet's write the code first:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate input\n    if not isinstance(case, dict) or 'lines' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: missing 'lines'\")\n    lines = case['lines']\n    if not isinstance(lines, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: 'lines' not a list\")\n    # optional: convert non-string to string? Assume strings\n    trajectory = []\n    error_line = None\n    prefix_map = {\n        'Thought:': 'thought',\n        'Action:': 'action',\n        'Action Input:': 'action_input',\n        'Observation:': 'observation',\n        'Final:': 'final'\n    }\n    allowed_next = {\n        None: {'thought'},\n        'thought': {'action', 'final'},\n        'action': {'action_input', 'observation'},\n        'action_input': {'observation'},\n        'observation': {'thought', 'final'},\n        'final': set()\n    }\n    last_type = None\n    for idx, line in enumerate(lines):\n        # Parse line\n        parsed_type = None\n        parsed_content = None\n        found = False\n        for prefix, t in prefix_map.items():\n            if line.startswith(prefix):\n                parsed_type = t\n                parsed_content = line[len(prefix):].strip()\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            error_line = idx + 1\n            break\n        # Check if allowed\n        allowed = allowed_next[last_type]\n        if parsed_type not in allowed:\n            error_line = idx + 1\n            break\n        # Valid record\n        trajectory.append({'type': parsed_type, 'content': parsed_content})\n        last_type = parsed_type\n    # If no violation detected inline, check end condition\n    if error_line is None:\n        valid_end_states = {None, 'observation', 'final'}\n        if last_type not in valid_end_states:\n            # last line causes incomplete cycle\n            error_line = len(lines)  # one-based\n            # remove the last record (the one that started the incomplete cycle)\n            if trajectory:\n                trajectory.pop()\n            valid = False\n        else:\n            valid = True\n    else:\n        valid = False\n    return {\n        'valid': valid,\n        'error_line': error_line,  # None if valid, else int\n        'trajectory': trajectory\n    }\n\nBut note: If error_line is None after loop but last_type is None (empty lines), valid true.\n\nNow, one more edge: If lines is empty, trajectory empty, loop doesn't run, error_line None, last_type None, valid true.\n\nNow, test with examples:\n\nCase 000: expect valid true, error_line null, trajectory: [{'type':'thought','content':'inspect'}, {'type':'action','content':'lookup'}, {'type':'observation','content':'found'}, {'type':'thought','content':'answer'}, {'type':'final','content':'done'}]. That seems correct.\n\nCase 001 and 002: violation at line 3, trajectory first two records.\n\nOur code will produce that.\n\nNow, we should also consider if lines contain \"Action Input:\" lines. For example: Thought, Action, Action Input, Observation, Thought, Action, Observation, Final. That should be valid. Let's simulate: lines: [\"Thought: a\",\"Action: b\",\"Action Input: c\",\"Observation: d\",\"Thought: e\",\"Action: f\",\"Observation: g\",\"Final: h\"]. Process:\nidx0 thought -> last_type thought\nidx1 action -> last_type action\nidx2 action_input -> allowed from action -> ok, last_type action_input\nidx3 observation -> allowed from action_input -> ok, last_type observation\nidx4 thought -> allowed from observation -> ok, last_type thought\nidx5 action -> allowed from thought -> ok, last_type action\nidx6 observation -> allowed from action -> ok, last_type observation\nidx7 final -> allowed from observation -> ok, last_type final\nEnd, last_type final -> valid true. Good.\n\nNow, what about \"Action Input:\" line without preceding Action? e.g., [\"Thought: a\",\"Action Input: b\"] -> after thought, allowed {action,final}, action_input not allowed -> violation at line2. Good.\n\nNow, implement as Python source string. We'll output JSON with single key \"implementation\": \"<escaped source>\"\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes (none in source except maybe in string literals?), newlines as \\n, double quotes as \\\". We'll produce a single line string.\n\nWe'll write the code without triple quotes in source? We'll use single quotes for strings to avoid escaping double quotes in JSON. But careful: JSON string must be enclosed in double quotes, so any double quote inside must be escaped. Our Python code uses double quotes for dictionary keys? Actually we use single quotes in the code above for strings (like 'lines', 'error_line', etc.) That will avoid double quotes inside. The only double quotes are in prefix_map keys like \"Thought:\", but those are single quotes in the code we wrote? Actually we used single quotes for 'Thought:' - that is fine. We'll keep all strings in Python with single quotes to minimize escaping. But the final output JSON will have a string value that contains Python source. In that source, we need to escape any double quotes if present. Since we use single quotes throughout, there will be no double quotes inside. However, there might be double quotes in docstring? We'll omit docstring. Also in the allowed_next dictionary, we used None key, no quotes. That's fine.\n\nOne more: In the prefix_map, we have 'Action Input:' which contains a single quote? No, it's 'Action Input:' with single quotes. So fine.\n\nThus, we can write the source exactly as above.\n\nNow, we need